{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011ChLUZLZQEQTnVvwsbe8iS","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01S7FWbXEJuHgVkARgdGmszB","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"sed -n 25,32p docs/TODO.md; python3 - <<'EOF'\np='docs/proposal.md'\ns=open(p).read()\na=\"the table holds no agent text or host paths.\"\nassert s.count(a)==1\ns=s.replace(a,a+\" Scanner failure streaks (#221, the early slice of the digest, #58): each scanner posts one comment on the status issue when a failure streak starts and one when it recovers. A streak starts at the first run that is a guard trip or guard error (reason: the guard rule name or `scan-error`), any other `scan_failed` run (a `timeout` counts as `scan_failed`) or a skip at `max_open`, and ends at the next `ok` run. The posted class is kept in the scanner's `meta` table (key `failure_streak`), so a restart does not repost, and it is written only after the post succeeds. Runs dropped at the global cap and `error` runs neither start nor end a streak. A comment carries the scanner name and the reason class only, never finding text, titles, fingerprints or log lines. The post goes through the effect layer, so a dry-run logs it (`scanner.streak_notice`) and stores no streak. While the status issue number is unknown nothing is posted and the streak is not recorded, so it posts once the issue exists.\")\nopen(p,'w').write(s)\nEOF"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4439,"cache_read_input_tokens":38792,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":4439},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"959b1387-657d-4b49-b132-2cc6ddeb0162","uuid":"a89be8f6-9aae-42fc-89dc-c